Undoubtedly there are many more bugs/enhancement needed, but these are the big ones:

Clipping issues occur on the first draw, which are corrected after resizing
	Resizing triggers the clip region to be regenerated.  This bug is most easily
	seen at the bottom of text input controls (see guido, tab 1)  Text input
	controls are actually a nested set of controls, and the initial clipping
	set-up appears to not be "deep", so the sub-controls don't get their clipping
	adjusted during creation.	

TabView contents can draw outside of the tab view bounds (see guido, tab 3)
	You can click the edges of the Walter logo to move it around and see it
	"escape" the tab view.  When running guido on Haiku, it clips to the tab view
	as you would expect.

Menus are implemented via a quick hack
	They don't draw using the normal BView draw methods yet, nor show shortcuts, nor
	allow you to traverse from one menu to another without re-clicking, nor do they
	support submenus.

Closing any window causes the app to crash (now has hacky workaround in window.c)
	See surface_leave() in window.c

Cairo asserts on exit due to non-empty object hash (i.e. something didn't get disposed)

TextEdit controls are editable but don't show a blinking cursor or selection rectangle
	Possibly a MessageRunner issue -- see below.
	Also the TextEdit control never get visibly focused like it should
	Selecting text works but is not shown in any way
	You have to force a refresh (e.g. by resizing the window) to see any text changes

BBitmap offscreen windows are not yet supported
	Wayland doesn't seem to support the concept of hidden windows, so in theory we
	would need to create a window structure with all of the Cairo backend, but none
	of the Wayland hooks.

MessageRunner works when targeting some views and not for others
	I can't seem to find a pattern for what works and what doesn't, but the behavior is 
	consistent at least.

Tooltips are never removed once shown
	They are also just a quick hack to get things working, and as with menus,
	and don't draw in the normal BView Draw() fashion either.

Apps using Layout are much more likely to crash

Scrolling of text views is only partially working (see AboutSystem)

Some Be apps set their initial window size to 0,0,0,0 and it gets resized correctly somehow
	I haven't figured out the mechanism that triggers Haiku to handle that.   Terminal and 
	Pairs do (or did) this, until I edited the size to make it work on Cosmoe.

BeOS/Haiku draws a gray (216, 216, 216) background color for windows, but Cosmoe uses white
	I can't find where this happens in Haiku -- the topview appears to use ViewColor() like any
	other view, and all view states default to white backgrounds in Haiku.

Alert are sometimes shown too small to fit their contents

Asynchonous alerts hang the program
	Synchronous alerts called with Go(NULL) work, but the async ones called with Go() cause a hang.

Spawning an alert sometimes cause a crash

Modal alerts are not actually modal
	Weston doesn't seem to support modal alerts at this point anyway

BChannelSlider crashes on Draw()

BColumnListView somehow accesses view states already popped and deleted by PopState()
	Somehow triggered by its CONSTRAIN_CLIPPING_REGION define, which I have now turned off.
	Eventually, this leads to a segfault.  This may be fixed as of 11/25/2025, but can't verify.

Clicks in a scrolled view are received at the equivalent unscrolled coordinates within the view
	For example, if you have scrolled the view down 50 pixels and click in it, the click will
	register 50 pixels too high.

B_CMAP8 (and a few others) are not valid color spaces for drawing, due to lack of support in Cairo
	Should we auto-upgrade these color spaces to B_RGB32 at BBitmap creation, to avoid issues?
	You can still set a BBitmap's bits with data from B_CMAP8 or other unsupported-for-drawing color
	spaces -- it will get correctly converted to the BBitmap's supported color space.

Pop-up menus don't update their selected item on-screen
	Pop-up menus work, in that they pop, selections can be made, and the correct command is issued.
	However, the newly selected item is not drawn on-screen, even after forcing a refresh.
	See Gradients app for an example.

Pairs draws just an empty window
	The Pairs app used to work quite well, but now it just draws an empty window, and by
	empty I mean literally empty -- completely transparent.


The following were known issues from Cosmoe before forking to support Wayland:

entry_ref's only work if Name holds an absolute path
	Converting from a BEntry or GetNextRef fills this out correctly, but considering how
	extensively entry_ref is used, it's certain this is causing issues somewhere.  I don't
	know of a specific problem though.

send_data() and receive_data() use a static, hard-coded 512 byte area to pass information
	The current implementation was a quick hack to get menus working, since they use this
	functionality extensively. I've never gone back and coded it to allocate memory dynamically.
	See src/system/kernel/thread.cpp

BVolume and BVolumeRoster need improvement
	BVolumeRoster gets filled out, but it's surprisingly tricky on Linux to get a list of only the 
	volumes a user would typically expect to see on a desktop.  So you'll end up with say /swapfile,
	but not	your mounted USB drive, for example.

Unit tests aren't even close to 100% passing
	We are now synced with Haiku unit tests.  Unfortunately, they use a custom unit test library that
	is a pain to work with, since determining exactly where a test failed is very time-consuming.
	I know this will be hugely valuable in making Cosmoe more stable and compliant though.

Cosmoe stubs out Haiku's BCatalog system for translating strings
	I think this would work fine if the Haiku catalogs were merged and installed, and the BCatalog
	(and friends) source was synced up with Haiku, but I just haven't seen this as a priority yet.

